          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/2008/06/nhibernate-thinks-your-enum-is-dirty/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: NHibernate Thinks Your Enum is Dirty">NHibernate Thinks Your Enum is Dirty</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Jun</span>
            <span class="day">30</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/category/net/" title="View all posts in .Net" rel="category tag">.Net</a>, <a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/category/nhibernate/" title="View all posts in NHibernate" rel="category tag">NHibernate</a> | <a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/2008/06/nhibernate-thinks-your-enum-is-dirty/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on NHibernate Thinks Your Enum is Dirty"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="710 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2008/06/30/nhibernate-thinks-your-enum-is-dirty.aspx">No Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Get your head out of the gutter. It&#8217;s important to look at your mapping files closely when trying to diagnose unexpected results.&nbsp; One of our developers had recently added two properties to our big honkin&#8217; CustomerOrder entity.&nbsp; These both happened to be enumeration values, which are represented by Int values in the database.&nbsp; Of course, [...]</p>

